# RALLY — external credentials
#
# RALLY is a static PWA (GitHub Pages): there is no server, so keys are
# entered IN THE APP and stored on-device, never committed. This file
# documents what each credential is and where it goes.
#
# ── Property data (territory door import) ─────────────────────────────
# REGRID_API_KEY=your-regrid-token-here
#   What: Regrid parcel API token — unlocks owner names, parcel numbers,
#         year built, lot size and sale history on imported doors.
#   Get it: https://regrid.com → sign up → API token.
#   Where to paste: RALLY → More → Property data → "Regrid API token".
#   Without it: the app uses OpenStreetMap building data automatically —
#   real houses, real addresses where mapped, no owner data. Zero setup.
#
# ── Map imagery ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GOOGLE_MAPS_KEY=your-google-maps-key
#   What: Google Map Tiles API key for satellite/hybrid imagery.
#   Status: an office key ships built in (data.js DEFAULT_GOOGLE_KEY) —
#   restricted by site + API on the Google Cloud project, which is the
#   correct protection for a browser map key.
#   Where to paste an override: More → Google map imagery.
#
# ── Apple MapKit JS (future) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# APPLE_MAPS_TEAM_ID=
# APPLE_MAPS_KEY_ID=
# APPLE_MAPS_PRIVATE_KEY=
#   MapKit JS requires a server that signs short-lived JWTs with the
#   private key — a static PWA cannot hold the key safely. When RALLY
#   gets its backend, a /mapkit-token endpoint signs tokens with these
#   and the map layer can offer Apple basemaps. Until then MapLibre +
#   Google imagery is the shipping map (deliberately: swapping the map
#   engine would rewrite pins/territories/routes/heat for zero user
#   benefit today).
#
# ── FieldRoutes CRM (future customer-history sync) ────────────────────
# FIELDROUTES_SUBDOMAIN=
# FIELDROUTES_KEY=
# FIELDROUTES_TOKEN=
#   Where to paste: More → FieldRoutes connection. Customer history on
#   the property card currently matches this device's own book; the
#   CRM provider interface (js/crm.js) is where the office API plugs in.
